12247	{"artifactId":"LLD-8b5693528b025a55-s1","entry":{"id":"migrated-LLD-8b5693528b025a55-s1-review","actor":{"id":"insrc:daemon","kind":"agent","properties":{}},"timestamp":"2026-10-22T11:56:56.239Z","operation":"review","outcome":{"result":"block","summary":"review: block","data":{"kind":"review","report":{"artifact":"design.story","stage":"design.story","verdict":"block","findings":[{"claimId":"coverage-of-intent.1","ref":"coverage-of-intent","item":"coverage-of-intent","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"The intent ('make the daemon update source (repo URL and branch) configurable') is designed for both update entry points, the daemon.update IPC helper and the CLI/TUI update, with absent or empty keys keeping today's behaviour, and nothing beyond that is built (the installer and other scripts are left alone).","evidence":"The LLD covers launchUpdate (src/daemon/update-runner.ts:136), runDetachedUpdate and its argv entry (:224, :285-292), maintenance.ts update() (src/cli/services/maintenance.ts:77) and daemon-ctl.sh (--repo-url added; --branch already exists at scripts/daemon-ctl.sh:321). Those are the only update paths: a grep for launchUpdate, runDetachedUpdate and the maintenance imports turns up no others. The installer (scripts/insrc-daemon-install.sh) already has --repo and --branch and is not redesigned. [files: src/daemon/update-runner.ts, src/cli/services/maintenance.ts, scripts/daemon-ctl.sh]","action":"none","fixability":"manual","outcome":"holds"},{"claimId":"current-behaviour.1","ref":"current-behaviour","item":"current-behaviour","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"Today daemon-ctl.sh sync_repo defaults BRANCH to HEAD's branch and runs fetch, then discards package-lock.json, then checks for a dirty tree, then merge-base --is-ancestor and an ff-only merge, exiting 3 on a dirty tree or a diverged HEAD. maintenance.ts mirrors this with `status --porcelain`.","evidence":"scripts/daemon-ctl.sh:82-109: `git fetch --quiet origin \"$branch\"`, `checkout -- package-lock.json`, `diff --quiet || diff --cached --quiet` → die ... 3, `merge-base --is-ancestor` → die 'has diverged' 3, `merge --ff-only`. cmd_start and cmd_update (:252, :288) use `${BRANCH:-$(git rev-parse --abbrev-ref HEAD)}`. maintenance.ts:86-117 follows the same order, with `status --porcelain` at :100. [files: scripts/daemon-ctl.sh, src/cli/services/maintenance.ts]","action":"none","fixability":"manual","outcome":"holds"},{"claimId":"current-behaviour.2","ref":"current-behaviour","item":"current-behaviour","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"The daemon.update IPC passes no flags today: the helper is spawned as `node <execArgv> update-runner.js <daemonCtl> restart`, and the entry reads argv[2] and argv[3].","evidence":"update-runner.ts:159-161 `[...process.execArgv, __filename, daemonCtl, 'restart']`. The entry at :285-288 reads `process.argv[2]` (daemonCtl) and `process.argv[3] ?? 'restart'`. runDetachedUpdate at :238 spawns `bash [daemonCtl, subcommand]` with no other arguments, so taking extra flags from argv[4..] is consistent. [files: src/daemon/update-runner.ts]","action":"none","fixability":"manual","outcome":"holds"},{"claimId":"current-behaviour.3","ref":"current-behaviour","item":"current-behaviour","kind":"semantic","severity":"HIGH","premise":"(Implicit in the branch switch and the edge case 'branch exists only at origin → `git checkout -B <branch> origin/<branch>` creates it from the fetched ref'.) After `git fetch origin <branch>`, the installed checkout has a remote-tracking ref origin/<branch> for any branch.","evidence":"The installer that creates ~/.insrc/daemon clones with a single branch: scripts/insrc-daemon-install.sh:191 `git clone --quiet --branch \"$BRANCH\" --single-branch \"$REPO_URL\" \"$INSTALL_ROOT\"`, and nothing widens remote.origin.fetch afterwards (a grep for set-branches and remote.origin.fetch finds nothing). In such a clone the configured refspec only maps the cloned branch. `git fetch origin <other>` therefore writes FETCH_HEAD only and does not create refs/remotes/origin/<other>. As a result, the existing `rev-parse \"origin/$branch\"` (daemon-ctl.sh:98, maintenance.ts:105) and the planned `checkout -B <branch> origin/<branch>` both fail on a standard install whenever the configured branch differs from the one installed, which is the main use case of the Story. Switching the URL to a fork has the same problem for any branch other than the installed one. [files: scripts/insrc-daemon-install.sh, scripts/daemon-ctl.sh, src/cli/services/maintenance.ts]","action":"The LLD must state how a branch outside the clone's refspec is fetched: either fetch with an explicit refspec (`git fetch origin +refs/heads/<b>:refs/remotes/origin/<b>`), or run `git remote set-branches --add origin <b>` before the fetch. Apply the same in maintenance.ts. Add a single-branch clone to the integration fixtures.","fixability":"manual","outcome":"does-not-hold"},{"claimId":"new-versus-reuse.1","ref":"new-versus-reuse","item":"new-versus-reuse","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"There is no existing daemonUpdate config reader or key, so readUpdateSource and the daemonUpdate.* catalog rows are new. The existing --branch flag and UpdateOptions.branch are reused as stated.","evidence":"A grep for daemonUpdate, readUpdateSource and UpdateSource in src/ finds nothing. UpdateOptions.branch already exists (maintenance.ts:43) and daemon-ctl.sh already parses --branch (:321). Note: the per-domain config readers live in src/config (loadLocalProviderConfig in src/config/local.ts:70, loadAnalyzeConfig in src/config/analyze.ts:260), so src/config/update-source.ts would match that convention better than src/shared. This does not affect correctness. [files: src/config/local.ts, src/cli/services/maintenance.ts]","action":"Consider placing the reader in src/config beside the other config readers.","fixability":"manual","outcome":"holds"},{"claimId":"change-sites.1","ref":"change-sites","item":"change-sites","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"Flags appended after the subcommand (`daemon-ctl.sh restart --repo-url X --branch Y`) are accepted by the script's argument parser.","evidence":"scripts/daemon-ctl.sh:315-326 parses every argument in a single while/case loop. The subcommand and the flags can come in any order, and an unknown argument dies with exit 1, which fits the stated exit 1 for a malformed --repo-url. [files: scripts/daemon-ctl.sh]","action":"none","fixability":"manual","outcome":"holds"},{"claimId":"change-sites.2","ref":"change-sites","item":"change-sites","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"The UpdateOptions inventory is complete: the only callers of update() are the services facade and the DaemonPane, so adding repoUrl as an optional field breaks no caller.","evidence":"src/cli/services/index.ts:44 declares `update(opts: UpdateOptions, onLog)`. The only call is src/cli/panes/DaemonPane.tsx:61 `svc.daemon.update({}, task.push)`, so opts.branch is never set by the TUI today and the config branch will normally apply. The tests import other maintenance exports (runUpdateReconcile, restart), not update(). [files: src/cli/services/index.ts, src/cli/panes/DaemonPane.tsx]","action":"none","fixability":"manual","outcome":"holds"},{"claimId":"data-compatibility.1","ref":"data-compatibility","item":"data-compatibility","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"Adding daemonUpdate.repoUrl and daemonUpdate.branch as CONFIG_CATALOG rows with empty-string defaults is safe: the reconcile fills them, leaves keys it does not know alone, and the existing catalog tests adapt.","evidence":"config-catalog.ts:77 onward: rows of the shape `{ path, type:'string', default, desc, group }` with group 'General' (see the ollama.host row at :80). reconcile.ts:328 reconcileConfig migrates, fills catalog defaults and prunes only RETIRED_PATHS (:331, :398). settings-catalog.test.ts:34 compares against CONFIG_CATALOG.length dynamically, so no count needs updating. [files: src/config/config-catalog.ts, src/config/reconcile.ts, src/config/__tests__/settings-catalog.test.ts]","action":"none","fixability":"manual","outcome":"holds"},{"claimId":"error-paths.1","ref":"error-paths","item":"error-paths","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"An invalid config is refused by launchUpdate before anything is spawned and before the marker is written.","evidence":"launchUpdate (update-runner.ts:145-182) resolves the helper and checks the lock before it spawns at :159 and writes the marker at :178. If the reader is called before the spawn, a throw leaves no child and no marker, exactly as the resolveDaemonCtl failure does today. The existing test 'launch failure spawns nothing' (update-runner.test.ts:139) is the model for the new test. [files: src/daemon/update-runner.ts, src/daemon/__tests__/update-runner.test.ts]","action":"none","fixability":"manual","outcome":"holds"},{"claimId":"error-paths.2","ref":"error-paths","item":"error-paths","kind":"semantic","severity":"MED","premise":"A dirty tree is refused before the remote URL changes ('the remote URL is not changed before this check'), while origin is set 'before the fetch'.","evidence":"Today the dirty check runs AFTER the fetch: daemon-ctl.sh:85 fetch, :90 package-lock discard, :92 dirty check. maintenance.ts has the same order (:91 fetch, :99-100 discard and porcelain check). Because the contract puts `git remote set-url` before the fetch, the stated guarantee only holds if the dirty check, together with the package-lock.json discard that precedes it, moves ahead of set-url. Neither 2.4 nor 2.5 says so. Built as written (set-url inserted before the existing fetch), a dirty tree would still have its origin rewritten before the exit 3. [files: scripts/daemon-ctl.sh, src/cli/services/maintenance.ts]","action":"State the new order in 2.4/2.5 for both implementations: discard package-lock.json, dirty check, set-url, fetch, local-only commit check, then checkout -B or the ff-merge.","fixability":"manual","outcome":"does-not-hold"},{"claimId":"tests.1","ref":"tests","item":"tests","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"The single acceptance criterion ac1 maps to concrete unit and integration tests, and script-level integration tests against the real daemon-ctl.sh are an established pattern.","evidence":"The acceptance mapping lists four tests for ac1. src/cli/services/__tests__/daemon-ctl-forced-stop.test.ts already runs scripts/daemon-ctl.sh against a temporary home, so daemon-ctl-source.test.ts is feasible. maintenance.ts reads DAEMON_ROOT from INSRC_DAEMON_ROOT when the module is imported (:37), so the test must set the variable before a dynamic import, as steering-refresh-through-daemon.test.ts:29 does. [files: src/cli/services/__tests__/daemon-ctl-forced-stop.test.ts, src/cli/services/__tests__/steering-refresh-through-daemon.test.ts]","action":"In maintenance-source.test.ts, set INSRC_DAEMON_ROOT (and HOME, because update() then runs the reconcile and steering refresh) before dynamically importing maintenance.js.","fixability":"manual","outcome":"holds"},{"claimId":"tests.2","ref":"tests","item":"tests","kind":"semantic","severity":"MED","premise":"The integration fixtures exercise the shape of checkout that the update runs against in practice.","evidence":"The fixtures are 'a temporary bare remote and clone created with git in the test', which is a normal full clone. The real install is `git clone --single-branch` (scripts/insrc-daemon-install.sh:191). In that shape, switching to another branch fails because origin/<branch> is never created (see current-behaviour). A full-clone fixture would pass and hide that failure. The edge cases 'repoUrl equals origin's URL → no set-url' and 'branch only at origin' also have no named test. [files: scripts/insrc-daemon-install.sh]","action":"Clone the fixture with --single-branch (as the installer does) and add cases for a branch other than the cloned one and for an unchanged URL.","fixability":"manual","outcome":"does-not-hold"}],"counts":{"high":1,"med":2,"low":9,"unverified":0},"template":"design-spec","reviewedAt":"2026-10-22T11:56:56.239Z","model":"cli-claude:opus","reviewedBy":"daemon"},"resolutions":{}}}}}
